WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.230
Lifetimes are Rust's way of reasoning
about reference validity.

2
00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:05.395
A reference must not outlive the data it
points to.

3
00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:07.113
That rule prevents a function, struct,

4
00:00:07.193 --> 00:00:10.221
or caller from keeping a reference to a
value that has already been moved,

5
00:00:10.301 --> 00:00:12.039
dropped, or otherwise made unavailable.

6
00:00:12.119 --> 00:00:14.353
Most lifetime reasoning is inferred
automatically.

7
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:15.376
In everyday Rust code,

8
00:00:15.456 --> 00:00:18.683
developers often write references without
explicit lifetime annotations.

9
00:00:18.763 --> 00:00:22.089
The compiler can usually determine how
long those references are valid based on

10
00:00:22.169 --> 00:00:23.956
scope, ownership, and how values are used.

11
00:00:24.036 --> 00:00:27.113
That inference keeps common code readable
while still enforcing safety.

12
00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.867
Lifetime annotations appear when
relationships among references need to be described

13
00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:31.493
explicitly.

14
00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:33.459
An annotation does not make data live
longer.

15
00:00:33.539 --> 00:00:36.965
It tells the compiler that one reference's
validity is related to another value's

16
00:00:37.045 --> 00:00:37.491
lifetime.

17
00:00:37.571 --> 00:00:38.912
If the relationship is not true,

18
00:00:38.992 --> 00:00:41.921
the compiler rejects the code rather than
allow an invalid reference.

19
00:00:42.001 --> 00:00:44.532
Returning references requires a valid
owner relationship.

20
00:00:44.612 --> 00:00:47.938
A function cannot safely return a
reference to a temporary local value that will

21
00:00:48.018 --> 00:00:49.458
disappear when the function ends.

22
00:00:49.538 --> 00:00:52.765
It can return a reference tied to an input
if the input owner lives long enough.

23
00:00:52.845 --> 00:00:56.121
It can also return owned data when the
function creates something new that must

24
00:00:56.201 --> 00:00:57.194
survive after the call.

25
00:00:57.274 --> 00:00:58.862
Lifetime errors are design feedback.

26
00:00:58.942 --> 00:01:02.318
They often mean ownership is unclear, a
reference is trying to outlive its owner,

27
00:01:02.398 --> 00:01:04.830
or a function is promising more than it
can safely provide.

28
00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:07.293
Good refactoring options include returning
owned data,

29
00:01:07.373 --> 00:01:10.054
keeping the owner alive longer,
restructuring data ownership,

30
00:01:10.134 --> 00:01:12.020
or avoiding references to temporary
values.
